      "text": "To find \\(\\sin F\\) in the right triangle \\(DEF\\), we start by analyzing the given information and the properties of right triangles.\n\n1. **Identify the right triangle and the given angles:**\n   - \\(DE\\) is opposite angle \\(D\\),\n   - \\(DF\\) is opposite angle \\(E\\),\n   - \\(EF\\) is the hypotenuse.\n   - \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\).\n\n2. **Determine the angle \\(E\\):**\n   - Since \\(\\sin E = 1\\), angle \\(E\\) must be \\(90^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\(EF\\) is the hypotenuse and \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) are the legs of the right triangle.\n\n3. **Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the lengths of the legs:**\n   - Let \\(DE = a\\) and \\(DF = b\\).\n   - Given \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), we have \\(\\frac{a}{EF} = \\frac{5}{13}\\). So, \\(a = \\frac{5}{13} \\cdot EF\\).\n   - Since \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) are the legs and \\(EF\\) is the hypotenuse, by the Pythagorean theorem:\n     \\[\n     a^2 + b^2 = EF^2\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(a = \\frac{5}{13} \\cdot EF\\):\n     \\[\n     \\left(\\frac{5}{13} \\cdot EF\\right)^2 + b^2 = EF^2\n     \\]\n     Simplifying:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{25}{169} \\cdot EF^2 + b^2 = EF^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     b^2 = EF^2 - \\frac{25}{169} \\cdot EF^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     b^2 = \\frac{169}{169} \\cdot EF^2 - \\frac{25}{169} \\cdot EF^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     b^2 = \\frac{144}{169} \\cdot EF^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     b = \\frac{12}{13} \\cdot EF\n     \\]\n\n4. **Determine \\(\\sin F\\):**\n   - Since angle \\(F\\) is the remaining angle in the right triangle, angle \\(F = 90^\\circ - E = 90^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 0^\\circ\\).\n   - However, we need to find \\(\\sin F\\). In a right triangle, the sine of the right angle is always 1. Therefore:\n     \\[\n     \\sin F = \\sin 90^\\circ = 1\n     \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin F\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
